hi everyone i am anketh  i completed my masters in scientific  computing and  i worked in industry in various roles as  a data scientist  product manager and a software developer  throughout this journey my core focus  has always been to help people  accelerate their data analytics and  scientific computing pipeline  and today i'll be talking about number  which is one such  tool which can help you supercharge your  scientific computing code in python  to begin with we researchers love python  because it is easy to learn the codes  written  are usable and readable it has a vast  ecosystem of more than 250 000 plus  contributed packages it takes fewer  lines of code  to implement new algorithms and build  prototypes  and scale them a feature rich jupiter  notebook  ecosystem allows us to have an  interactive environment  where we can write our codes  mathematical equations  display visualizations and add narrative  text  this even promotes reproducible research  and scientific transparency  and the list goes on and on  but when i was pursuing my masters six  years back  working on transport phenomena solving  multiple differential equations  i was hit very hard by the fact that  when it comes to performance  python implementation was magnitudes  slower when compared to traditional  languages  like fortran and c plus which made me  question if learning python is so easy  why is increasing the performance of my  python code  so difficult  thankfully times have changed a lot of  effort  has been made in the last decade and two  to accelerate python  you can have a look at some of the  alternative implementations  and packages which aid acceleration in  this need for speed timeline  broadly there are two ways to speed up  your python codes  one is by using a replacement python  interpreter  like pipey which is difficult to  implement which has its own ecosystem of  python packages  which is significantly smaller and  limited  the other popular alternative is to use  python to see  c plus plus transpilers like nuetca  python and cyton they have limited  static analysis  and ahead of time generated code is  either bloated  or underspecialized also both these  alternatives do not address non-cpu  targets like gpus  which are gaining widespread adoption in  the scientific computing community  and then there is one library number  which stands out in this crowd  it is easy to learn use and implement  just like learning python and it often  provides performance gains  comparable to traditional programming  languages  as there is no ahead of time compilation  step  so using it is straightforward and you  can directly plug it in  into your jupyter notebook environment  so what is number it is an open source  jit compiler  which translates a subset of python and  numpy code into fast machine code  all you need to do is add a jit  decorator  to your python function and you will  gain performance similar to cc  plus and fortran without switching  languages  or interpreters to understand how number  works  we can quickly go through the process  which happens  behind the scene so first you add the ng  decorator  to the function you want to accelerate  engine stands for  jit in no python mode which is the  preferred mode  to supercharge your code as there is  zero involvement  of the python interpreter in the  compiled code and this leads to the best  performance  when the function is invoked for the  first time in the session  a byte code analysis of the python  function is performed based on which  a number intermediate representation is  generated  the function arguments are also analyzed  to infer the data types  post which the ir is rewritten this  high level and abstract number ir  goes through the process of lowering  which  converts it into low level and machine  specific  ln vmir based on the cpu  or gpu target the llvm or nvvm  compiler infrastructure is used to  generate  optimized machine code which is executed  if you pass cache equal to true option  then it will persist the compiled code  on your disk which can be used  across sessions  so let's get started with our first  example we will compute the mandelbrot  set  a set of points on the complex plane  which always remains bounded  by a threshold value while solving the  quadratic recurrence equation  it is an iterative problem which is also  very compute intensive in nature  the first step is to import the  requisite libraries  which we will be using throughout this  exercise  next we will set up the domain size as  a 500 cross 500 grid and limit  the maximum iteration for each point on  the grid  to 1000  now let us write a pure python function  to evaluate the mandelbrot set  and return a nested list of color tuples  for the entire domain since we are using  only python  we will use the data structures  available natively  we will initialize a nested list of  tuples  denoting the color of each pixel on the  complex plane  next we will traverse the complex plane  using nested for loops  here y denotes the index along the  imaginary axis  and x denotes the index along the real  axis  the complex number corresponding to each  grid point is initialized  and we begin the iterative computation  when c breaches the threshold value  after a certain  number of iterations the corresponding  color tuple is evaluated using a  mathematical function  finally the function returns the color  of the entire grid  let us now execute the function  you can see it took around seven seconds  to execute the entire function  we can visualize the generated  mandelbrot set using the python image  link library  it looks fascinating now let us see if  we can  speed up the entire computation using  number  all we need to do is create a copy of  the above function  and add the num budget decorator  let us execute the function wow  it took less than a second now let us  execute it again  the performance improved even further  since we did not provide any  specification regarding the function  input types in the ng decorator number  does a lazy  compilation of the function when the  function is executed for the first time  it does the type inferencing and  compiles the code which is directly used  for all successive execution  we test the results and yeah it looks  good  so we got an enormous performance boost  just by applying the engine decorator  but is that  it no number also provides you with  certain options  that can enhance the performance even  further  all you need to do is switch the  parallel option to true  and number will generate codes capable  of  parallel execution on multiple threads  in this case we will also specify the  for loop  we want to execute in parallel using the  p range function available in number  so i have parallelized the loop  execution along the y  axis so let us now execute the function  amazing it took less than 100  milliseconds this time  you can clearly see the improvement in  performance  is even further so you can inspect the  default number of threads  that are being used by number using the  number num threads  config variable in my case it is 16  and you can also set the number of  threads which can be used  by number using the set num threads  function  let us set the number of threads as 4  and execute it  some other options available in the nj  decorator are fast math  and cache fast matter reduces the  numerical rigor  which can help you gain further  improvement in performance  also you can persist the compiled code  on disk  which can help you avoid the overhead  you experience  in the first run  now let us move to the next example  where we will use  numpy array as the data structure you  can see the code logic  is exactly the same as we had in the  pure python version  if you are new to numpy you are and you  are not familiar with vectorization  and array expressions you will tend to  use for loops to access  and modify arrays in this function i am  doing the same  now let me execute the function  you can witness the function is not up  to the mark as  accessing numpy arrays using for loops  is quite expensive  let us see if number comes to our rescue  we once again create a copy of the  function and add the ng decorator  after executing the function we can see  that the performance gain is quite  significant and  slightly better than what we achieved  using the  pure python code now  let us switch the parallel option to  true and add the period function  and execute it  this time the performance gain is quite  significant  because though numpy is primarily  designed  to be high performant on single core  number  automatically compiles a version which  can run  in parallel if it contains reduction  functions  array math functions and many more  functions  assignments and operations  you are well versed in numpy universal  functions and vectorization  number can still help you speed up your  scientific computing pipeline  as it allows you to write u funcs in  python  instead of c and directly compiling into  numpy ufunk there is a numpy counterpart  vectorized decorator  which vectorizes a python function  but it is just for simplicity and it is  neither optimized  nor efficient  so let us first start with the numpy  vectorize decorated function  you can witness its performance is  slightly better than the nested for  loops  once we switch to the number vectorized  decorator we are able to compile it into  a u-func  which performs significantly better  to add icing to the cake you can also  create a u-func which can execute  in parallel using the target option  which increases the performance even  further  as we can see here  moving to the final example we will  solve the diffusion equation where a  steel square block  is at an initial temperature of 300  kelvin and  it is subjected to 700 kelvin boundary  temperature  we will solve the differential equations  using central difference method  in space and forward difference method  in time  we will set up the domain provide the  initial conditions and define the  thermal properties  the number of time steps we will  undertake is 500  and the you not specifies the  temperature field at time t equal to  zero  using for loops we have a readable code  now  let us go ahead and execute the function  it takes around 12 seconds to execute we  can plot the temperature field and  inspect it  now let us rewrite the function using  numpy vectorized expression  this totally eliminates the need for for  loops  after executing the code we are able to  obtain 200 times faster performance  but the sacrifice is huge in terms of  code readability  the more complex the equation the more  difficulty we will have  in understanding the code  number provides a stencil decorator  which comes to a rescue  a stencil is a computational pattern  which  automatically generates the looping code  required for a neighborhood  it allows clearer more concise and high  performance code  we start by defining a kernel which  computes the equation we want to  apply on the domain and add the stencil  decorator  in case the kernel is not applicable on  the boundaries  the output value of the boundaries are  defaults to zero  this can be changed via c val parameter  as we have specified in this case which  is t  hot or 700 kelvin now  let us go ahead and execute the code  the performance gain is more than 700  times  which clearly demonstrates how you can  supercharge your scientific computing  code  using number without hampering the code  readability  to summarize it is a good idea to use  number  for numerical algorithms which are arrow  oriented  math heavy have lot of loops  when the data is in the form of numpy  arrays  when the performance bottlenet is a  handful of well  encapsulated functions you can use  number  to create universal functions in python  that can be broadcasted over numpy  arrays instead of writing them using c  apis  you can also use number to generate  parallel codes targeting multi-core cpus  and gpus and as we have already seen you  can create  fast and clean codes using the stencil  kernels  now let us check out a real life open  source project which has benefited from  number acceleration  the name of the project is blur hash  number which is the fastest python 3  blurage implementation  you can go ahead and check out the  project source  and the interactive demo on github.com  animator slash blurage number it is also  available as a python package  on piping  so blurrage is actually quite popular  and used in popular sites such as reddit  medium as a placeholder while the  network image is still loading  so bloodrush algorithm takes an image  and gives a short string which is only  20 to 30 characters that represents the  placeholder for this image  so the process of encoding is basically  conversion of an image  into a blura string and the process of  decoding  is the conversion of this blur hash  string into a blurred  image  i will now share some performance  testing results for both  the encoding and decoding  implementations and compare it to the  pure python blur rash implementation  as compared to pure python  implementation  in blurage number there was a speed up  of  around 33 to 63 times while encoding an  image  with sizes ranging from 128 across 128  pixels to 204 it crossed two zero four  red pixels  also the process of decoding saw a  performance boost of 34 to 70 times  for the same target image sizes  such performance gains can have a  tremendous financial impact and help you  save money  when you are running image processing  algorithms like blur hash  on serverless frameworks where you are  built based on the function usage  which mostly includes the runtime  in case you are already using dusk or  rapids  number integrates with them seamlessly  also you can install number using berry  countdown raspberry pi  and speed up edge device computation for  example machine learning scoring  or any other s device analytical  computation  i have personally tried it and it works  really well  the manual broad set example that we  executed today  was also executed on raspberry pi and  the performance gain was  more than 20 times with this we have  come to an end to this talk  if you have any questions please feel  free to ask me in the q a session  you can find the notebooks used in this  talk on my website which is real  worldpython.guide  so thank you for attending this talk and  have a nice day  you

